{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-827bdcac4eb2ce2f","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-9020-E001\n3\ttitle: AMD Instinct validation campaign for vLLM ROCm serving parity\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9020\n5\tcomponent: Serving Runtimes\n6\tteam: RHOAI Serving\n7\ttype: Investigation\n8\tpriority: P0\n9\tdependencies: []\n10\tinvestigation_signals:\n11\t  question_specificity: 1\n12\t  source_accessibility: 0\n13\t  local_runnability: 0\n14\t  cluster_hardware_dependence: -2\n15\t  human_judgment_required: -1\n16\timplementation_type: null\n17\tai_signals: null\n18\tai_implementability: Low\n19\tai_implementability_score: -2\n20\tjira_key: null\n21\tbranch: null\n22\tgated_by: null\n23\tgate_failure_impact: null\n24\t---\n25\t# AMD Instinct validation campaign for vLLM ROCm serving parity\n26\t\n27\t## Description\n28\t\n29\tThis epic resolves the strategy's central unknown: whether vLLM serving on AMD Instinct accelerators reaches functional and performance parity with the productized CUDA builds for the platform's supported model set. It runs a validation campaign on physical AMD Instinct hardware and produces the parity data plus a recommended go/no-go productization scope per model — the outcome that determines the scope of the vLLM ROCm serving runtime productization. The epic's scope also includes securing access to dedicated AMD Instinct nodes (procurement or partner-lab arrangement).\n30\t\n31\t## Scope\n32\t\n33\t- Secure access to dedicated AMD Instinct hardware for the campaign (procurement or partner-lab); the granting of access is externally controlled and tracked as a risk, while everything the campaign produces is within this epic's control.\n34\t- Define the validation matrix: the supported model set, functional pass/fail criteria per model, and the performance parity baselines measured against the productized CUDA runtime builds.\n35\t- Execute vLLM serving validation on ROCm for every model in the supported model set, using upstream ROCm-enabled vLLM builds.\n36\t- Measure performance parity data per model against the CUDA baseline.\n37\t- Publish the campaign report: per-model functional results, performance parity data, and a recommended go/no-go productization scope for each validated model.\n38\t\n39\t## Acceptance Criteria\n40\t\n41\t- A validation plan exists that enumerates the supported model set, the functional pass/fail criteria, and the performance parity baselines against the CUDA runtime builds.\n42\t- The campaign report is published with functional validation results for every model in the supported model set.\n43\t- The campaign report includes performance parity data per model, measured against the CUDA baseline on comparable hardware.\n44\t- The campaign report states a recommended go/no-go productization scope for each validated model.\n45\t\n46\t## HLR Traceability\n47\t\n48\t- HLR-1 (P0): full coverage — the validation campaign on physical AMD Instinct hardware establishing functional and performance parity data for vLLM serving of the supported model set, and producing the go/no-go scope for productization.\n49\t\n50\t## Risks\n51\t\n52\t- Hardware access (procurement or partner-lab) is unresolved and controlled by parties outside the strategy; it may delay the campaign but does not change the campaign's deliverables.\n53\t- Upstream ROCm image cadence is controlled by AMD and the upstream communities; the campaign validates against the upstream ROCm builds available at execution time.\n54\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-84efe7eb08293406","agent_id":"ac4f4c6601cc7f52f","session_id":"c382ed88-b964-4c48-b2ae-a143f546fdbd","uuid":"3aa6fa7d-282f-4a10-a6f4-ab3461f41c71","timestamp":"2026-10-26T11:39:05.157Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-827bdcac4eb2ce2f","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-9020 decomposition"}
