{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b5c41e221810f65b","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstrat_id: RHAISTRAT-9020\n3\tscore: 12\n4\tpass: false\n5\trecommendation: revise\n6\tissues:\n7\t- severity: major\n8\t  criterion: Type Correctness\n9\t  description: 'E006 lists Investigation epic RHAISTRAT-9020-E001 in its dependencies\n10\t    but sets gated_by: null. The campaign outcome rewrites E006 core content (HLR-4\n11\t    validated model list is the campaign direct output; on the no-models-clear path\n12\t    the serving docs content collapses with E002 halt scenario). Rule 3 requires every\n13\t    epic depending on an Investigation to record the gate; the decomposition did so\n14\t    for E002 (gated_by + rewrite impact) but not E006, and automated consumers detect\n15\t    gates from gated_by. Fix: set gated_by=RHAISTRAT-9020-E001 on E006 with gate_failure_impact\n16\t    (action: rewrite; fallback: narrow the validated-model-list content to the cleared\n17\t    set and defer serving-runtime docs if nothing clears), or remove the direct E001\n18\t    dependency, its DAG edge, and its justification row if the model list is consumed\n19\t    transitively via E002.'\n20\t- severity: minor\n21\t  criterion: DAG Coherence\n22\t  description: The E001-to-E006 edge justification row in the decomposition summary\n23\t    cites Rule 11, but Rule 11 covers Implementation-to-docs-authoring edges and E001\n24\t    is an Investigation; the edge is actually justified by its content dependency\n25\t    (the validated model list). Mislabelled rule citation only; the edge itself does\n26\t    not serialize parallel work.\n27\terror: null\n28\t---\n29\t## Review Summary\n30\t\n31\tScore: 12/14 — fail\n32\tRecommendation: revise\n33\t\n34\tContract validator: OK (all mechanical checks passed: body sections, canonical components, Rule 11 docs-last coverage, priority inheritance, Rules 7/8/10 parallelism).\n35\t\n36\tTriage consistency: `triage` is null (full decomposition) with `epic_count: 6` — consistent; multi-epic output is expected.\n37\t\n38\tThe decomposition is strong on six of seven criteria: complete HLR coverage with correct docs-authoring priority handling, a clean and correctly-parallel DAG, clean component/team boundaries, well-calibrated AI signals verified against `.context`, testable deliverable-rooted ACs with externally-controlled preconditions correctly filed as risks, and full strategy-scope coverage. It fails on one gating-metadata defect: E006 depends on the Investigation epic E001 without recording the gate, while the identical dependency on E002 was correctly gated. Per the auto-fail rule, a criterion at 0 forces `pass: false` regardless of the total score.\n39\t\n40\t## Criterion Details\n41\t\n42\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n43\t\n44\t- HLR-1 (P0) → E001; HLR-2 (P0) → E002 + E003; HLR-3 (P1) → E004 + E005; HLR-4 (P2) → E006. Every HLR appears in the corresponding epics' HLR Traceability sections; the traceability matrix is present with no orphaned requirements.\n45\t- Priority inheritance: E001 (P0) gates E002 (P0) — correct. E004/E005 (P1) block only E006, a docs-authoring epic exempt from priority inheritance. E006 is P1 via the docs-authoring exception (strategy priority Major → P1), correctly recorded in Health Warning 5 so it is not an inversion.\n46\t- No priority collapse: no epic bundles a deferrable lower-priority HLR with P0 work (E006 maps solely to HLR-4; E002/E003 map solely to HLR-2).\n47\t\n48\t### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n49\t\n50\t- Frontmatter/DAG cross-check: all six edges (E001→E002, E001→E006, E002→E006, E003→E006, E004→E006, E005→E006) appear identically in the summary diagram and the target epics' `dependencies` lists, in both directions. Arrows are drawn dependency→dependent per convention. No circular dependencies. Critical path length 3 (E001→E002→E006) is within the M-standard heuristic (3-4).\n51\t- Rule 10 verified by enumeration: E003 (`konflux-onboarding`) has no `dependencies` edge to any general implementation epic (E002, E004, E005); its coupling to E002 is expressed as the \"built and green through the platform build pipeline\" AC on E002 — the rule-compliant shape. No license-validation or repo-onboarding epics exist, so Rules 7/8 are vacuous.\n52\t- Rule 11 verified: E006 is blocked by all four Implementation epics.\n53\t- E004 ∥ E005 is correct (separate repos, no shared build-against artifact); the quota configuration E004 validates against is treated as a configuration reference / AC gate per Rule 13, not an edge.\n54\t- No missing data/artifact edges discoverable from epic content: the pipeline output E002 consumes is a build-pipeline output consumed as content, which is correctly an AC gate rather than a DAG edge.\n55\t- The go/no-go outcome is correctly handled as a rewrite gate on E002 rather than conditional branch files — the outcome space (any subset of the model set clearing) is not ≤3 bounded outcomes, so no `-BRANCH-` files are required.\n56\t- Minor: the E001→E006 justification row cites \"Rule 11\", but Rule 11 covers Implementation→docs-authoring edges and E001 is an Investigation; the edge is actually justified by its content dependency (the validated model list). Mislabelled citation only — the edge does not serialize parallel work (E001 already precedes E006 transitively via E002).\n57\t\n58\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n59\t\n60\t- Component/team tuples are clean: E001/E002/E003 = Serving Runtimes / RHOAI Serving; E004 = Training Operator / Distributed Workloads; E005 = Distributed Workloads / Distributed Workloads; E006 = Documentation / RHOAI Serving. All component values are canonical.\n61\t- The E004/E005 split of HLR-3 follows the component boundary (Rule 1) with the shared end-to-end validation as an AC on E004 rather than an edge — correct.\n62\t- KServe is treated as a passive component with explicit rationale and a re-scope trigger in the Component Mapping Notes; its validation is an AC on E002, owned by the same team (RHOAI Serving owns KServe per architecture context). Acceptable — a no-work KServe epic would be worse.\n63\t- No scope duplication: E002 (runtime productization) and E003 (pipeline onboarding) are distinct work types coupled only via an AC, not two builds of the same surface.\n64\t- No work-product mixing: E006 is pure content authoring; no epic bundles code with content.\n65\t- Sizes are within the ~2-week guideline. E001 is one logical work item (a single campaign over the enumerated model set) — watch note only, no split demanded.\n66\t\n67\t### 4. Type Correctness (0/2)\n68\t\n69\t- E001 is a genuine Investigation: its outcome (the cleared model set) determines E002's scope and existence, and downstream dependents exist. Correctly typed.\n70\t- E002: `gated_by: RHAISTRAT-9020-E001` is in its direct `dependencies`, and `gate_failure_impact` has both `action: rewrite` and a fallback approach. Correctly recorded as a true gate.\n71\t- **Major issue — E006 lists the Investigation epic E001 in its `dependencies` with `gated_by: null`.** The campaign's outcome rewrites E006's core content: HLR-4's validated model list is the campaign's direct output, and on the no-models-clear path (E002's halt scenario) the serving documentation content collapses with it. Rule 3 requires every epic that depends on an Investigation to record the gate (`gated_by` plus `gate_failure_impact`), and the decomposition applied exactly that to E002 while omitting it on E006 — an internal inconsistency, not a judgment call. It also has a functional cost: automated pipeline consumers detect gates from `gated_by`, so E006's gate is invisible to them. Fix: either set `gated_by: RHAISTRAT-9020-E001` on E006 with `gate_failure_impact` (action: rewrite; fallback: narrow the validated-model-list content to the cleared set and defer serving-runtime docs if nothing clears), or remove the direct E001 dependency — and its DAG edge and justifica