{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8c892b0eed99333b","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9020\n3\tepic_count: 6\n4\tcritical_path_length: 3\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\t---\n9\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9020\n10\t\n11\t**Strategy:** AMD GPU (ROCm) enablement for serving and training (Major, Refinement Done)\n12\t\n13\t**Triage:** Full decomposition (not below-threshold, not docs-only). The strategy spans four components, two teams, and P0/P1/P2 requirements, and contains a genuine unknown — the validation campaign's go/no-go outcome — that determines the scope of the P0 productization work.\n14\t\n15\t## Epic List\n16\t\n17\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n18\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n19\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E001 | AMD Instinct validation campaign for vLLM ROCm serving parity | Investigation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n20\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E002 | Productize supported vLLM ROCm serving runtime | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n21\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E003 | Konflux build pipeline for the vLLM ROCm runtime image | Implementation (konflux-onboarding) | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n22\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E004 | PyTorch ROCm training support for PyTorchJob | Implementation | Distributed Workloads | P1 |\n23\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E005 | Kueue quota handling for amd.com/gpu | Implementation | Distributed Workloads | P1 |\n24\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E006 | AMD GPU (ROCm) enablement documentation | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI Serving | P1 |\n25\t\n26\t## Dependency DAG\n27\t\n28\t```mermaid\n29\tgraph TD\n30\t    E001[\"E001 Validation campaign (Investigation)\"] --> E002[\"E002 vLLM ROCm runtime productization\"]\n31\t    E001 --> E006[\"E006 Documentation\"]\n32\t    E002 --> E006\n33\t    E003[\"E003 Konflux pipeline (parallel to E002)\"] --> E006\n34\t    E004[\"E004 PyTorch ROCm training\"] --> E006\n35\t    E005[\"E005 Kueue amd.com/gpu quota\"] --> E006\n36\t```\n37\t\n38\tArrows point from dependency to dependent (E001 must complete before E002 starts).\n39\t\n40\t## DAG Justification\n41\t\n42\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n43\t|------|------|-----------|\n44\t| E001 → E002 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | The campaign's go/no-go outcome determines which models the productized runtime covers — HLR-2 is explicitly scoped to \"the models the validation campaign clears.\" True gate: `rewrite` (scope changes with the cleared model set; if nothing clears, productization is removed). |\n45\t| E001 → E006 | Rule 11 + content dependency | The documentation's validated model list is a direct output of the campaign; docs cannot be authored before the list exists. |\n46\t| E002 → E006 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Documentation describes the productized runtime. |\n47\t| E003 → E006 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Documentation describes the released, pipeline-built runtime variant. |\n48\t| E004 → E006 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Documentation covers ROCm training support. |\n49\t| E005 → E006 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Documentation covers amd.com/gpu quota usage. |\n50\t\n51\t**Non-edges (mandated or justified parallelism):**\n52\t\n53\t| Pair | Rule | Rationale |\n54\t|------|------|-----------|\n55\t| E003 ∥ E002 | Rule 10 (konflux-onboarding ∥ general implementation) | Pipeline onboarding and runtime productization run in parallel; the coupling is expressed as the \"build pipeline green\" acceptance criterion on E002, never as a DAG edge. |\n56\t| E003 ∥ E001 | Independent inputs | The pipeline recipe does not depend on which models clear validation. |\n57\t| E004 ∥ E005 | Rule 14 (different repos, no shared artifact) | Training Operator and Kueue are separate midstream repos; the end-to-end \"admitted through Kueue with amd.com/gpu quota\" validation is an acceptance criterion on E004, with the quota configuration treated as a configuration reference (AC gate), not a build-against artifact. |\n58\t| E004, E005 ∥ E001, E002, E003 | Rule 17 / independent work streams | Training enablement (HLR-3) delivers independent partial value and is not gated by the serving validation campaign's outcome or the serving runtime's productization. |\n59\t\n60\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n61\t\n62\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Notes |\n63\t|-----|----------|---------|-------|\n64\t| HLR-1 | P0 | E001 | Direct mapping: the validation campaign and its go/no-go output are the epic's deliverable. |\n65\t| HLR-2 | P0 | E002, E003 | E002 carries the requirement (runtime + ServingRuntime productization, gated on the campaign's cleared model set); E003 is the build-pipeline enablement of the same deliverable, running in parallel per Rule 10. |\n66\t| HLR-3 | P1 | E004, E005 | Split along the component boundary (Training Operator vs. Distributed Workloads — Rule 1), same owning team, parallel repos (Rule 14). Both epics are P1, matching HLR-3; neither enables P0 work, so no priority inheritance applies. |\n67\t| HLR-4 | P2 | E006 | Docs-authoring epic. Its priority is P1, not P2, by the docs-authoring priority exception (strategy frontmatter priority Major → P1); this is intentional, not an inversion. |\n68\t\n69\tAll four HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n70\t\n71\t## Component Mapping Notes\n72\t\n73\t- **KServe** is listed as an affected component but the strategy implies no KServe code change — serving on AMD GPUs is delivered by the ROCm runtime image plus GPU resource scheduling, and the InferenceService/ServingRuntime surfaces already support arbitrary resource names. KServe is therefore treated as a **passive component**: its validation (\"an InferenceService using the ROCm vLLM runtime deploys and serves on AMD Instinct nodes\") is an acceptance criterion on E002, the nearest active component's epic, owned by the same team (RHOAI Serving) — no separate validation epic. If KServe code changes emerge during execution, re-scope.\n74\t- **E004/E005 split of HLR-3:** the single requirement \"PyTorch training on ROCm … admitted through Kueue, with amd.com/gpu quota handling\" spans two canonical components owned by the same team. Split per Rule 1 (different component → separate epic); the shared end-to-end validation is an AC on E004, not an edge.\n75\t- **E002/E003 component:** both map to `Serving Runtimes` (canonical name), covering the strategy's Serving Runtimes and (passively) KServe entries.\n76\t- **E006 component:** `Documentation` (canonical name); the strategy names no docs owner, so the epic is assigned to RHOAI Serving (the strategy's primary owning team) — see Health Warnings.\n77\t\n78\t## Health Warnings\n79\t\n80\t1. **Missing strategy sections** (non-blocking): the Affected Components section is a bare list (no per-component change or owner detail); there is no Impacted Teams table, no Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then Acceptance Criteria, no NFRs, and no technical approach section. Teams were derived from the architecture-context owning teams (RHOAI Serving for KServe/Serving Runtimes; Distributed Workloads for Training Operator/Distributed Workloads). Proceeded with available information.\n81\t2. **Hardware access (open question 1)** is an availability precondition, not an epic or an acceptance criterion: procurement or partner-lab access to dedicated AMD Instinct nodes is granted by parties outside the strategy. It is captured as a risk on E001 (whose scope includes *securing* access) and as a shared validation constraint on E004/E005; the campaign's ACs sit on its deliverable (the validated report), per the externally-controlled-precondition rule.\n82\t3. **Upstream ROCm image cadence (open question 2)** is an external timing risk on E002, not a structural unknown: upstream vLLM/ROCm builds exist, and cadence misalignment delays release timing without changing which epics exist or what they do. Handled via tiered delivery (below) and an AC gate on the supported upstream base image; no DAG edge and no separate upstream epic (no PR/RFC contribution from us is required).\n83\t4. **Docs epic ownership assumption:** the strategy names no documentation owner; E006 is assigned to RHOAI Serving. Content spans both serving and training areas — confirm the owning team before scheduling.\n84\t5. **Docs priority vs. HLR priority:** HLR-4 is P2 but E006 is P1 (docs-authoring exception: strategy priority Major → P1). Recorded here so the review does not flag it as an inversion.\n85\t\n86\tNo priority inversions and no scope traps were identified beyond the above. All epics are within the ~2-week epic-size guideline; the validation campaign (E001) is one logical work item (a single campaign over the supported model set) and is not split.\n87\t\n88\t## Tiered Delivery\n89\t\n90\tThe upstream ROCm build cadence (AMD-controlled, uncertain timing) gates only the productized serving runtime, and partial value can be delivered without it:\n91\t\n92\t- **Tier 1 (independent value, start immediately):**\n93\t  - E001 — validation campaign (risk-reduction; produces the go/no-go data)\n94\t  - E003 — Konflux pipeline for the ROCm image variant (release-train-independent)\n95\t  - E004, E005 — PyTorch ROCm training support and amd.com/gpu quota handling (HLR-3; uses upstream ROCm-enabled PyTorch images directly)\n96\t- **Tier 2 (gated):**\n97\t  - E002 — productized vLLM ROCm serving runtime (HLR-2), gated by the campaign's cleared model set and by availability of a supported upstream ROCm base image aligned to the release train\n98\t  - E006 — documentation, last per Rule 11 (Tier 2 by construction since it is blocked by all Implementations)\n99\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b04dfde947081c54","agent_id":"a75bd94d3d7d66008","session_id":"c382ed88-b964-4c48-b2ae-a143f546fdbd","uuid":"06548588-efbe-4541-a139-12aab17d1d47","timestamp":"2026-10-26T11:46:26.627Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-8c892b0eed99333b","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","